"""Hand-written English method descriptions, part 3: equipment Machine.

Keyed by the v5 catalog slug (slugify of the canonical exercise name).
Merged by build_progression.py as the last description tier; a key that
matches no catalog row fails the build, so typos surface immediately.
"""

DESCRIPTIONS = {
    "assisted_pull_ups": "The assisted pull up uses a counterweighted machine to remove part of the bodyweight from the pull. Grip width and cues stay the same as a regular pull up; lighten the assist gradually over weeks and the first unassisted rep tends to follow.",
    "chest_supported_smith_machine_row": "The chest-supported Smith machine row sets an incline bench under the bar so the chest stays pinned while the arms row. The pad removes all cheating from the pull; squeeze the shoulder blades at the top and let the elbows drive past the ribs.",
    "close_stance_leg_press": "The close-stance leg press places the feet hip-width and low on the platform, shifting the emphasis toward the quads. Keep the lower back glued to the pad and stop the set when the pelvis starts to tuck; depth is useful, lumbar rounding is not.",
    "high_foot_leg_press": "The high-foot leg press places the feet high and wide on the platform so the hips and hamstrings do most of the pushing. The knees travel less and the glutes load hardest at depth; expect it to feel closer to a hinge than a squat.",
    "hip_adduction": "The machine hip adduction squeezes the legs together against weighted pads, training the adductors directly. Keep the movement smooth through the full travel; the stretch at the wide position is where most of the value lives.",
    "horizontal_leg_press": "The horizontal leg press pushes a weighted sled along a rail from a reclined seat, loading the legs without compressing the spine. Keep the whole foot flat and the pelvis on the pad; the range should end before the hips curl off the seat.",
    "machine_assisted_dips": "The machine assisted dip kneels or stands on the counterweight platform of an assisted dip station and performs dips with part of the bodyweight removed. Set the assist as low as honest reps allow; the movement pattern and elbow position stay identical to the free-weight dip, which makes it the standard bridge toward full dips.",
    "machine_back_extension": "The machine back extension trains the spinal erectors through a controlled hinge and raise against a weight stack. Hinge from the hips, keep the ribs down and squeeze the glutes to finish; the movement should come from the hips, not a whip of the lower back.",
    "machine_bicep_curl": "The machine bicep curl locks the elbows into a pad and moves the handle through a fixed arc, isolating the biceps. The pad removes all swing; let the arm fully straighten at the bottom and squeeze at the top of each rep.",
    "machine_hip_abduction": "The machine hip abduction pushes the legs apart against a weighted pad, isolating the glute medius and upper glutes. Sit tall with the chest up and control the return; a slight forward lean changes the feel but should be deliberate, not a slump.",
    "machine_seated_crunch": "The machine seated crunch loads a crunch through a padded lever, letting the abdominals train against a measurable stack. Curl the ribs toward the pelvis and exhale hard at the top; the weight should move because the trunk rounds, not because the arms pull.",
    "plate_loaded_donkey_calf_raise": "The plate-loaded donkey calf raise bends the torso forward and drives the heels up against a pad on the lower back, putting the calves under a long, heavy stretch. Let the heels sink deep at the bottom and pause at the top; the donkey angle places tension on the soleus and gastrocnemius alike.",
    "plate_loaded_glute_drive": "The plate-loaded glute drive is a hip thrust machine: the back pads against a bench-style support while the feet drive the platform up through hip extension. The machine handles setup and stability so the glutes can be loaded progressively and heavily.",
    "plate_loaded_lateral_raise": "The plate-loaded lateral raise isolates the side delts through a machine's fixed arc with lever arms or a loaded plate. The track removes momentum from the equation; raise to shoulder height and control the descent rather than swinging.",
    "plate_loaded_shrug": "The plate-loaded shrug traps the shoulders against a machine's padded levers so the traps lift a heavy load straight up. Shrug up and back, pause briefly at the top, and let the arms stay long; the traps, not the biceps, move the weight.",
    "seated_smith_machine_shoulder_press": "The seated Smith machine shoulder press presses the guided bar overhead from an upright bench. The vertical track removes stabilization work, so the delts and triceps can be loaded strictly; set the bench so the bar descends just in front of the face.",
    "single_arm_machine_shoulder_press": "The single-arm machine shoulder press presses one handle overhead from a fixed seat, training each side on its own. The machine stabilizes the path so the working shoulder can focus on pressing; keep the torso upright and resist the twist toward the loaded side.",
    "single_arm_plate_loaded_lateral_raise": "The single-arm plate-loaded lateral raise raises one lever at a time through the machine's arc, letting each delt work without the stronger side hiding. Keep the elbow slightly bent and the torso still; lighter than the two-arm version is normal.",
    "smith_machine_bulgarian_split_squat": "The Smith machine Bulgarian split squat sets the rear foot on a bench with the bar guiding a perfectly vertical path, letting the front leg take all the load. The fixed bar removes balance from the equation, so more load reaches the quads and glutes of the working leg.",
    "smith_machine_good_morning": "The Smith machine good morning hinges at the hips under a guided bar on the back, loading the hamstrings and erectors through a controlled bow. The fixed path makes the pattern safer to learn than the free-bar version; keep the knees soft and the spine long.",
    "smith_machine_hip_thrust": "The Smith machine hip thrust drives the hips to full extension against a bar that travels straight up over them. The fixed path removes the balancing act of the free-bar version, so the glutes can be loaded hard and progressively.",
    "smith_machine_reverse_grip_bent_over_row": "The Smith machine reverse-grip bent-over row rows the guided bar with palms up, shifting emphasis toward the lats and lower biceps. The fixed path takes balance out of the hinge; keep the torso close to parallel and pull the bar to the lower ribs.",
    "smith_machine_reverse_lunge": "The Smith machine reverse lunge steps back under a guided bar, keeping the load traveling straight up and down. The fixed path spares the knees and lets balance take a back seat, which suits higher loads and stricter form.",
    "smith_machine_romanian_deadlift": "The Smith machine Romanian deadlift hinges under a bar locked to a vertical track, stretching the hamstrings under a guided load. The bar's path removes forward drift so the hinge stays honest; push the hips back and stop when the hamstrings run out.",
    "smith_machine_split_squat": "The Smith machine split squat holds a static split stance while the guided bar tracks straight over the midfoot. With balance handled by the machine, the front quad and glute take clean, progressive load.",
    "v_bar_lat_pulldown": "The V-bar lat pulldown uses a close neutral handle to pull the weight down to the chest, emphasizing the lower lats and a strong squeeze. Keep the torso near upright and drive the elbows down toward the hips rather than pulling with the hands.",
    "wide_stance_leg_press": "The wide-stance leg press spreads the feet high and wide on the platform, opening the hips toward the adductors and glutes. Depth still rules; go as deep as the pelvis allows without lifting off the pad, and drive through the whole foot.",
}
